    "q-129445": {
      "dom_id": "step-q129445",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z={x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2$ at the point $(1,2,2).$",
      "worked_solution": "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y)=(1,2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,2) & =3({1}^{2})({2}^{2})=12 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,2) & =2({1}^{3})(2)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-2 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,2)+(y-2)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,2) \\\\ z-2 & =12(x-1)+4(y-2) \\\\ z-2 & =12x+4y-20 \\\\ 12x+4y-z-18 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
