    "q-129435": {
      "dom_id": "step-q129435",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Determine the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-7$ at the point $(1,-1,6).$",
      "worked_solution": "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-7) \\\\  & =8x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-7) \\\\  & =18y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y)=(1,-1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,-1) & =8(1)=8 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,-1) & =18(-1)=-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-6 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,-1)+(y-(-1))\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,-1) \\\\ z-6 & =8(x-1)-18(y+1) \\\\ z-6 & =8x-18y-26 \\\\ 8x-18y-z-20 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
