    "q-129439": {
      "dom_id": "step-q129439",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Determine the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+3$ at the point $(0,1,1).$",
      "worked_solution": "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+3) \\\\  & =2x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+3) \\\\  & =-4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y)=(0,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,1) & =2(0)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,1) & =-4(1)=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-1 & =(x-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,1)+(y-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,1) \\\\ z-1 & =0x-4(y-1) \\\\ z-1 & =-4y+4 \\\\ 4y+z-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
