    "q-37721": {
      "dom_id": "step-q37721",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}}-y$ at the point $(3,2,3).$",
      "worked_solution": "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}}}\\cdot 2x-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}}}\\cdot 2(3{y}^{2})-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}}}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y)=(3,2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(3,2) & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+2(2)^{3}}}=\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(3,2) & =\\frac{3(2)^{2}}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+2(2)^{3}}}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{5}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-3 & =(x-3)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(3,2)+(y-2)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(3,2) \\\\ z-3 & =\\frac{3}{5}(x-3)+\\frac{7}{5}(y-2) \\\\ 5z-15 & =3(x-3)+7(y-2) \\\\ 5z-15 & =3x-9+7y-14 \\\\ -3x-7y+5z+8 & =0 \\\\ 3x+7y-5z-8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
