    "q-129483": {
      "dom_id": "step-q129483",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=z\\sqrt{y}-x$ at the point $(-1,4,1,3).$",
      "worked_solution": "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z\\sqrt{y}-x)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z\\sqrt{y}-x)=\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z\\sqrt{y}-x)=\\sqrt{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(-1,4,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(-1,4,1) & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(-1,4,1) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(-1,4,1) & =\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-3 & =(x-(-1))\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(-1,4,1)+(y-4)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(-1,4,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(-1,4,1) \\\\ w-3 & =-(x+1)+\\frac{1}{4}(y-4)+2(z-1) \\\\ w-3 & =-x+\\frac{1}{4}y+2z-4 \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{4}y-2z+w+1 & =0 \\\\ 4x-y-8z+4w+4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
