    "q-129554": {
      "dom_id": "step-q129554",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "At which points on the surface $z={x}^{3}+xy$ is the tangent plane horizontal?",
      "worked_solution": "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+xy)=3{x}^{2}+y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+xy)=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨3{x}^{2}+y,\\,x,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+y=0 \\\\ x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the second equation we get $x=0.$ Substituting $x=0$ into first equation, we obtain $y=0.$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz={0}^{3}+0(0)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,0).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
