    "q-73863": {
      "dom_id": "step-q73863",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "At which points on the surface $z={x}^{2}+2x+{y}^{2}+2y$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector  $v\\,=\\,⟨-4,0,1⟩?$",
      "worked_solution": "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2x+{y}^{2}+2y) \\\\  & =2x+2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2x+{y}^{2}+2y) \\\\  & =2y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨2x+2,\\,2y+2,\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨-4,0,1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨2x+2,\\,2y+2,\\,-1⟩=⟨-4,0,1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}c(2x+2)=-4 \\\\ c(2y+2)=0 \\\\ -c=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=-1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-(2x+2)=-4\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=-1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n-(2y+2)=0\\,⟹\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=1$ and $y=-1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =(1)^{2}+2(1)+(-1)^{2}+2(-1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(1,-1,2).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
