    "q-37990": {
      "dom_id": "step-q37990",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "At which points on the surface $z=4{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{3}-6$  is the tangent plane \nhorizontal?",
      "worked_solution": "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{3}-6) \\\\  & =8xy+{y}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{3}-6) \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨8xy+{y}^{3},\\,4{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2},\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}8xy+{y}^{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=0$ or ${y}^{2}+8x=0.$ So, we have two cases.\n\n- If $y=0$, substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+3x(0)^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\n- If ${y}^{2}=-8x$, substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+3x(-8x)^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ back into ${y}^{2}=-8x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=-8(0)\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nSo, in both cases, we have $x=0$ and $y=0.$ Finally, substituting these values into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=4(0)^{2}(0)+(0)(0)^{3}-6=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,-6).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
